Ladybird continues inline lines across columns even when they contain
floats. Floats are positioned as exclusions before the retained line
range is known, and only the floats belonging to that range are placed.
A non-fitting float resumes before the next column’s inline content.
Block-child walks also keep deferred floats in their continuation.

Blink’s PositionFloat in floats_utils.cc checks the available space,
returns a break-before token when a float must move, and prevents later
floats from overtaking it. Gecko’s BlockReflowState::FlowAndPlaceFloat
moves a non-fitting float with PushFloatPastBreak, independently of the
line content. WebKit accounts for floats in RenderBlockFlow::
computeLineAdjustmentForPagination when choosing a line’s column,
without making its whole inline formatting context unbreakable.

The issue’s five reductions match Chrome: Four-column containers are
210px tall, and the three-column floated list is 294px tall. Separate
controls cover deferred floats, clearance, and fixed-height columns.

Ladybird still treats the contents of each float as unbroken. All three
other engines can split a float across columns. Existing geometry cases
that require such splitting therefore remain different; this change
only adds continuation of whole floats and their surrounding content.

An empty soft-break record below a float is excluded from the widow
and orphan counts. Forced empty lines still count. This preserves
the required three-line orphan group when a full-width float moves
with the inline content, as in widows-orphans-021.html.
